the collaborative process itself can be targeted for
disruption (He et al., 2024; Yuan et al., 2024).
Current discourse on LLM-MAS security pri-
marily focuses on threats derived from agents, such
as misinformation (Ju et al., 2024) and jailbreak
attacks (Gu et al., 2024). The potential for these
attacks to propagate within the system exacerbates
the security challenges compared to single-agent
contexts (Dong et al., 2025b; He et al., 2025).
Recently, resource consumption attacks (RCAs)
against LLMs (Gao et al., 2024; Dong et al., 2025a)
have emerged as an urgent vulnerability similar to
traditional Denial-of-Service (DoS) attacks towards
computer networks (Lau et al., 2000; Schuba et al.,
1997). However, such RCAs fall short in capturing
the distinct vulnerabilities from multi-agent collab-
oration. Unlike DoS targeted network resources,
or single-LLM attacks that induce repetitive gen-
eration (Zhang et al., 2024b; Geiping et al., 2024),
the threat we identify operates at the level of agent
interaction. This attack turns the multi-agent col-
laboration into a mechanism of systemic failure.
To address this gap, we formalize a new threat
class we term Denial-of-Collaboration (DoC). DoC
attacks poison the communication of LLM-MASs,
rendering each agent inactive, so the system re-
mains functionally paralyzed, as illustrated in Fig.
1. As a practical instantiation, we further intro-
duce the COntagious Recursive Blocking Attack
(CORBA ), which operates by injecting instructions
to achieve DoC. The instructions induce the agent6899

to perform a blocking action and propagate the
complete instructions to all neighbors. By using
the instructions following and topology (Yu et al.,
2024), CORBA forces all agents into the infinite
loop, weaponizing the system’s communications.
Our experiments show that CORBA achieves an
attack success rate of 85% on average on two pop-
ular open-source LLM-MAS frameworks, Auto-
Gen (Wu et al., 2024) and Camel (Li et al., 2023).
In contrast, conventional prompt injection fail to
propagate systemically, a result that highlights the
novelty and potency of our contagious nature.
2 Related Works
LLM-Based Agent and Multi-Agent System.
LLM-based agents have made significant achieve-
ments (Yao et al., 2023; Shen et al., 2023; Liu et al.,
2023). By collaboration, the capabilities of multi-
agent systems are further extended, enabling them
to accomplish more complex tasks (Talebirad and
Nadiri, 2023; Chen et al., 2023; Baek et al., 2024;
Li et al., 2025). Some autonomous LLM-MASs
can even simulate human society (Hua et al., 2023;
Park et al., 2024; Piatti et al., 2024)
LLM Blocking Attacks. Some studies have noted
that malicious attackers can induce LLMs to gener-
ate redundant responses through specially crafted
prompts, leading to the consumption of computa-
tional resources (Geiping et al., 2024; Fu et al.,
2025). In white-box settings, such attacks typically
rely on training modifications or access to gradient
information (Gao et al., 2024; Dong et al., 2025a).
Optimization-based black-box approaches utiliz-
ing LLMs have also been proposed (Zhang et al.,
2024b). These works indicate the growing attention
to blocking attacks as a critical security concern.
3 Denial-of-Collaboration (DoC)
3.1 Preliminary
We model an LLM-MAS, denoted as S, comprising
a set of nagents, A={a1, a2, . . . , a n}. The com-
munication structure is represented by a directed
graphT= (A,E), where an edge (ai, aj)∈ Esig-
nifies that agent aiis permitted to send messages or
instructions to agent aj. We denote the response of
agent akat time step tto a prompt Pasr=at
k(P).
An attacker’s objective is injecting a malicious Pdoc
into an initial entry agent, ab∈ A, at time tm.
3.2 From DoS to DoC
It is crucial to distinguish DoC from conventional
DoS attacks on computer network. DoS typicallytargets localized node availability via Resource
Consumption Attacks (RCA) (Zhang et al., 2024b;
Dong et al., 2025a). In contrast, DoC targets the
integrity and utility of the collaborative process
itself . DoC weaponizes communications to induce
global unproductivity, rendering paralyzed despite
remaining superficially operational.
3.3 Formal Definition of DoC
We formalize the state of a system under a DoC at-
tack. The attack’s success is predicated on two key
properties: blocking individual agents and ensuring
the state is contagious across the topology. Agent
Blocking State. An individual agent aiis consid-
ered to be trapped in a blocked state with respect to
a malicious prompt Pdocif, from time tmonward,
its output is perpetually dictated by Pdoc, producing
a corresponding malicious response Rdoc. Formally,
for an agent aithat receives Pdocat time tm:
