4.2 Formal Definition of C ORBA
By combining these two core properties, we for-
mally define the C ORBA attack as follows: Defini-
tion 3 (Contagious Recursive Blocking Attack,
CORBA ): ACORBA attack commences when an
adversary injects a carefully crafted malicious in-
struction Pdocinto an entry agent ab∈ A at time
tm. The instruction Pdocis designed such that the
response Rdoc=at
i(Pdoc)produced by any receiv-
ing agent ai(fort≥tm) will simultaneously trig-
ger both self-sustainment andcontagion . Through
this recursive and contagious process, the block-
ing state propagates from the entry agent abalong
the paths of the communication graph Tto the
set of all reachable agents, R(ab). After a finite
time delay, every affected agent enters a perma-
nently blocked state, ultimately driving the entire
system, or its critical subsystems, into the state
ofDenial-of-Collaboration (DoC) as defined in
Eq. 2, thereby achieving the attack’s objective.
