In conclusion, CORBA is effective in multiple
experimental settings and can deceive existing safe-
guard detectors. We believe that the reason for
blocking prompts performing well is that it is only
feasible in LLM-MAS scenarios, and existing de-
fenses for LLMs are not very effective in detection.
6 Conclusions
This paper introduces CORBA , a malicious attack
paradigm designed to block LLM-MASs and de-
grade their availability. Due to the fact that the
attack prompts are harmless to individual LLMs,
it is challenging for existing defenses to identify
and defend against them. Extensive experiments
demonstrate the vulnerability of existing open-
source frameworks and open-ended simulations.
Our findings highlight the need for stronger secu-
rity measures, paving the way for the development
of more resilient and reliable LLM-MASs.6903

Limitations
Our study reveals the potential risk of blocking
attacks in existing LLM-MAS applications. And
we conducted experiments across various LLMs
and topologies. However, our focus is primarily
on exposing these vulnerabilities in LLM-MASs,
and few discuss developing strategies to mitigate
them. We confirm that the proposed mitigations
will benefit the application of LLM-MASs in the
real world. In light of the abnormal consumption of
resources caused by blocking attacks, monitoring
resources might be a promising method to protect
LLM-MASs. Consequently, in future works, we
will further investigate effective defense mecha-
nisms to prevent such blocking attacks in LLM-
MASs.
