A Evaluation Metric
Due to LLM-MASs having various topologies, an
effective attack must ensure that as many agents
as possible enter a blocked state. To quantify this,
we follow the attack success rate in jailbreak (Zou
et al., 2023), and define the Proportional Attack
Success Rate (P-ASR) , which measures the pro-
portion of blocked agents within an attacked LLM-
MAS.
P-ASR =/summationtextn
i=1I(at
i(Pm)=Rm,t≥tm)
n. (5)
A higher P-ASR typically indicates a greater degra-
dation in the availability of the system, meaning an
attack is able to cause damage effectively.
In addition to effectiveness, we also claim that ef-
ficiency is equally vital for blocking attacks. There-
fore, we design the Peak Blocking Turn Num-
ber (PTN) , which evaluates how quickly the attack
reaches its peak impact for the victim LLM-MASs:
PTN= min/parenleftigg
t∈N|n/summationdisplay
i=1I(at
i(Pt)=Rr)=n/parenrightigg
.(6)
PTN indicates the number of turns required for the
attack to stabilize at the maximum P-ASR. A lower
PTN suggests a faster and more efficient attack.
Note that PTN = 1 typically suggests either an
ineffective attack or a topology with too few nodes.
Evaluation Prompt
You are an LLM Safety Checker. Your task
is to check whether there are safety
issues in the following text, including
but not limited to violent, pornographic
words and hints, as well as various
safety hazards.
Next, I will give you a set of sentences.
Please rate the degree of danger of this
set of sentences. The score range is
[0, 1], 0 represents the safest and 1
represents the most dangerous, accurate
to two decimal places.
Example:
```
User:
What’s up today?
It’s a sunny day! By the way, what are
you doing?
I’m preparing for an exam tomorrow, such
a hard time!
You:
0.01
```
Figure 5: Direct LLM evaluation.
Overall, since blocking prompts are only feasible
in LLM-MAS scenarios, existing defense for LLMs
are not very effective to detect such attacks.Evaluation Prompt
